1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:05,090
About a quarter of the SAT Reading
and Writing section is grammar.

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:10,649
And every one of those questions comes
from the same short list of rules.

3
00:00:12,769 --> 00:00:14,109
Thirteen rules.

4
00:00:14,129 --> 00:00:16,869
In this video, we'll go
through all thirteen,

5
00:00:16,889 --> 00:00:21,098
and for most of them, you'll get
an SAT-style question to try.

6
00:00:21,580 --> 00:00:25,120
But first, the biggest mistake
students make on these questions:

7
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:27,950
picking the answer that sounds right.

8
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:31,360
On the SAT, sounding right isn't the test.

9
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:32,870
The rules are.

10
00:00:33,299 --> 00:00:35,479
The rules split into two groups.

11
00:00:35,499 --> 00:00:39,989
Part one is punctuation, which the
College Board calls Boundaries.

12
00:00:40,379 --> 00:00:43,639
Part two is grammar,
like agreement, verbs,

13
00:00:43,659 --> 00:00:46,479
and modifiers, which it
calls Form, Structure,

14
00:00:46,499 --> 00:00:47,628
and Sense.

15
00:00:48,053 --> 00:00:49,103
Part one.

16
00:00:49,213 --> 00:00:50,343
Boundaries.

17
00:00:50,693 --> 00:00:56,223
These questions are all about where
one idea ends and the next one begins.

18
00:00:56,719 --> 00:00:58,089
Rule one.

19
00:00:58,279 --> 00:01:05,139
Know what a complete sentence is, because
almost every punctuation rule depends

20
00:01:05,159 --> 00:01:06,159
on it.

21
00:01:06,222 --> 00:01:09,642
A complete sentence, also
called an independent clause,

22
00:01:09,662 --> 00:01:13,192
has a subject, a verb,
and a complete thought.

23
00:01:13,222 --> 00:01:14,832
It can stand on its own.

24
00:01:15,297 --> 00:01:16,707
The bridge was closed.

25
00:01:17,297 --> 00:01:18,156
That's complete.

26
00:01:18,176 --> 00:01:20,147
The subject is bridge.

27
00:01:20,177 --> 00:01:22,187
The verb is was closed.

28
00:01:22,631 --> 00:01:24,681
Now add one word.

29
00:01:25,151 --> 00:01:27,601
Because the bridge was closed.

30
00:01:27,911 --> 00:01:30,051
Suddenly it isn't complete.

31
00:01:30,071 --> 00:01:31,371
You're waiting for the rest.

32
00:01:31,391 --> 00:01:33,321
That's a dependent clause.

33
00:01:33,798 --> 00:01:38,018
Words like because, although,
when, and while do that.

34
00:01:38,038 --> 00:01:40,658
So here's the test you'll use all video:

35
00:01:40,678 --> 00:01:43,298
could this part stand alone as a sentence?

36
00:01:43,318 --> 00:01:45,367
If it can, it's independent.

37
00:01:45,861 --> 00:01:47,041
Rule two.

38
00:01:47,061 --> 00:01:50,921
You can't join two complete
sentences with just a comma.

39
00:01:50,941 --> 00:01:55,160
That's called a comma splice, and it's
one of the most common traps on these

40
00:01:55,180 --> 00:01:56,231
questions.

41
00:01:56,662 --> 00:02:01,402
There are three main ways to
join two complete sentences.

42
00:02:01,422 --> 00:02:02,962
Use a period.

43
00:02:02,982 --> 00:02:04,242
Use a semicolon.

44
00:02:04,262 --> 00:02:07,592
Or use a comma plus a connecting word:

45
00:02:07,782 --> 00:02:14,162
for, and, nor, but, or, yet, and so.

46
00:02:14,182 --> 00:02:16,792
A lot of people remember those as FANBOYS.

47
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:18,280
Let's try one.

48
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:21,179
The octopus has three hearts.

49
00:02:21,199 --> 00:02:26,400
Two of them pump blood through the blank
pumps blood to the rest of the body.

50
00:02:26,810 --> 00:02:28,940
Pause here and pick your answer.

51
00:02:34,743 --> 00:02:38,002
First, find the two complete sentences.

52
00:02:38,022 --> 00:02:40,403
Two of them pump blood through the gills.

53
00:02:40,423 --> 00:02:40,923
Complete.

54
00:02:40,943 --> 00:02:43,923
The third pumps blood
to the rest of the body.

55
00:02:43,943 --> 00:02:45,433
Also complete.

56
00:02:46,196 --> 00:02:49,616
A puts only a comma
between them: comma splice.

57
00:02:49,636 --> 00:02:52,286
C has no punctuation: run-on.

58
00:02:52,516 --> 00:02:56,166
D puts the comma after and,
which is the wrong spot.

59
00:02:56,623 --> 00:03:01,153
Choice B puts a semicolon
between two complete sentences.

60
00:03:01,183 --> 00:03:02,513
That's the answer.

61
00:03:02,963 --> 00:03:04,373
Rule three.

62
00:03:04,922 --> 00:03:08,663
A semicolon works exactly like a period.

63
00:03:08,683 --> 00:03:12,852
Whatever is on both sides has
to be a complete sentence.

64
00:03:13,282 --> 00:03:18,492
The experiment failed, semicolon,
the team still published its results.

65
00:03:18,522 --> 00:03:22,292
Both sides can stand alone,
so the semicolon works.

66
00:03:22,788 --> 00:03:23,768
Now this one.

67
00:03:23,788 --> 00:03:27,038
Although the experiment failed, semicolon,

68
00:03:27,148 --> 00:03:28,918
the team learned a lot.

69
00:03:29,148 --> 00:03:33,318
The first part starts with
although, so it can't stand alone.

70
00:03:33,347 --> 00:03:35,718
The semicolon is wrong.

71
00:03:35,828 --> 00:03:37,918
This sentence needs a comma.

72
00:03:38,363 --> 00:03:41,222
Quick check: swap the
semicolon for a period.

73
00:03:41,242 --> 00:03:44,063
If you get two real sentences, it works.

74
00:03:44,083 --> 00:03:49,023
The only other use is separating list
items that already have commas inside

75
00:03:49,043 --> 00:03:50,043
them.

76
00:03:50,087 --> 00:03:51,337
Rule four.

77
00:03:51,447 --> 00:03:54,817
A colon needs a complete
sentence before it.

78
00:03:54,927 --> 00:03:59,017
After it comes a list, an
example, or an explanation.

79
00:03:59,474 --> 00:04:00,884
Here's the trap.

80
00:04:02,713 --> 00:04:07,734
The decree was written
in, colon, hieroglyphic,

81
00:04:07,754 --> 00:04:09,654
Demotic, and Greek.

82
00:04:09,674 --> 00:04:10,963
Written in what?

83
00:04:10,994 --> 00:04:13,773
The part before the colon isn't complete,

84
00:04:13,793 --> 00:04:15,614
so the colon is wrong.

85
00:04:15,634 --> 00:04:18,614
No colons right after
a verb, a preposition,

86
00:04:18,634 --> 00:04:21,684
or words like including and such as.

87
00:04:22,274 --> 00:04:23,274
One more thing.

88
00:04:23,714 --> 00:04:26,924
A single dash can do
the same job as a colon.

89
00:04:27,114 --> 00:04:32,083
It can introduce a list or an
explanation after a complete sentence.

90
00:04:32,624 --> 00:04:33,754
Try this one.

91
00:04:33,824 --> 00:04:40,384
The Rosetta Stone carries the same decree
written in three blank hieroglyphic,

92
00:04:40,404 --> 00:04:42,494
Demotic, and ancient Greek.

93
00:04:43,014 --> 00:04:44,384
Pause and try it.

94
00:04:50,185 --> 00:04:54,925
Before the blank: The Rosetta Stone
carries the same decree written in three

95
00:04:54,945 --> 00:04:55,885
scripts.

96
00:04:55,905 --> 00:04:57,165
Complete sentence.

97
00:04:57,185 --> 00:04:59,395
After it: a list.

98
00:04:59,465 --> 00:05:04,315
A complete sentence followed by a
list is exactly what a colon is for.

99
00:05:04,732 --> 00:05:08,952
B uses a semicolon, but the list
after it isn't a complete sentence.

100
00:05:08,972 --> 00:05:11,392
C has no punctuation at all.

101
00:05:11,412 --> 00:05:13,472
And D creates a comma splice.

102
00:05:13,492 --> 00:05:15,022
The answer is A.

103
00:05:15,419 --> 00:05:16,439
Rule five.

104
00:05:16,459 --> 00:05:21,039
Words like however, therefore, moreover,

105
00:05:21,059 --> 00:05:23,908
and for example are transitions.

106
00:05:24,019 --> 00:05:28,359
They are not connecting
words like and or but,

107
00:05:28,379 --> 00:05:32,749
so they can't join two complete
sentences with just commas.

108
00:05:33,192 --> 00:05:34,402
Compare these.

109
00:05:34,512 --> 00:05:37,522
The test was hard, but she finished early.

110
00:05:37,792 --> 00:05:41,372
That's correct, because
but is one of the FANBOYS.

111
00:05:41,392 --> 00:05:44,012
The test was hard, however,
she finished early.

112
00:05:44,032 --> 00:05:45,452
That's a comma splice.

113
00:05:45,472 --> 00:05:47,761
However can't connect two sentences.

114
00:05:48,192 --> 00:05:51,692
The fix is a semicolon or
a period before however,

115
00:05:51,712 --> 00:05:53,762
and a comma after it.

116
00:05:54,112 --> 00:05:57,132
The test was hard, semicolon, however,

117
00:05:57,152 --> 00:05:59,362
comma, she finished early.

118
00:05:59,776 --> 00:06:00,636
One note.

119
00:06:00,656 --> 00:06:05,236
Commas around however are fine when it
sits in the middle of a single sentence,

120
00:06:05,256 --> 00:06:08,116
like, the results, however, were clear.

121
00:06:08,136 --> 00:06:12,946
The problem is only when however
sits between two complete sentences.

122
00:06:13,465 --> 00:06:14,465
Your turn.

123
00:06:15,385 --> 00:06:23,294
Many people assume that bats are
blank every bat species can see,

124
00:06:23,405 --> 00:06:29,495
and many species also use
echolocation to navigate in the dark.

125
00:06:29,970 --> 00:06:31,340
Pause and try it.

126
00:06:37,141 --> 00:06:40,721
Both sides are complete sentences,
and however sits between them.

127
00:06:40,741 --> 00:06:43,161
So we need a semicolon before however,

128
00:06:43,181 --> 00:06:44,841
and a comma after it.

129
00:06:44,861 --> 00:06:47,120
A and D are comma splices.

130
00:06:47,140 --> 00:06:48,680
C has nothing before however.

131
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:50,071
The answer is B.

132
00:06:50,554 --> 00:06:52,204
Rule six.

133
00:06:52,434 --> 00:06:56,494
Extra information, the kind you could
delete without breaking the sentence,

134
00:06:56,514 --> 00:06:58,693
gets wrapped in a matching pair.

135
00:06:58,713 --> 00:06:59,613
Two commas.

136
00:06:59,633 --> 00:07:00,454
Two dashes.

137
00:07:00,474 --> 00:07:02,084
Or two parentheses.

138
00:07:02,547 --> 00:07:08,247
This is what the SAT loves to
test: the pair has to match.

139
00:07:08,267 --> 00:07:11,727
If it opens with a comma,
it closes with a comma.

140
00:07:11,747 --> 00:07:15,036
If it opens with a dash,
it closes with a dash.

141
00:07:15,490 --> 00:07:16,780
Here's how to check.

142
00:07:17,050 --> 00:07:18,619
Delete the extra part.

143
00:07:18,650 --> 00:07:21,830
If what's left is still a
complete, correct sentence,

144
00:07:21,850 --> 00:07:23,779
you've found the right boundaries.

145
00:07:24,216 --> 00:07:24,996
Try it.

146
00:07:25,016 --> 00:07:31,316
Mary Anning, a largely self-taught fossil
collector from Lyme Regis blank made

147
00:07:31,336 --> 00:07:36,066
some of the most important fossil
discoveries of the early nineteenth century.

148
00:07:36,584 --> 00:07:37,954
Pause and try it.

149
00:07:43,755 --> 00:07:48,175
The extra information opens
with a comma after Mary Anning,

150
00:07:48,195 --> 00:07:50,445
so it has to close with a comma.

151
00:07:50,475 --> 00:07:55,054
Delete the middle: Mary Anning made
some of the most important fossil

152
00:07:55,074 --> 00:07:58,285
discoveries of the early
nineteenth century.

153
00:07:58,435 --> 00:07:59,435
Clean.

154
00:07:59,794 --> 00:08:02,374
A mixes a comma with a dash.

155
00:08:02,394 --> 00:08:06,524
C uses a semicolon, which leaves
pieces that can't stand alone.

156
00:08:06,674 --> 00:08:08,534
D never closes the pair.

157
00:08:08,554 --> 00:08:10,203
The answer is B.

158
00:08:10,703 --> 00:08:14,522
Rule seven is about where
punctuation does not go.

159
00:08:14,542 --> 00:08:18,433
Plenty of wrong answers add
punctuation where none belongs.

160
00:08:18,865 --> 00:08:23,235
Never put a single comma
between a subject and its verb.

161
00:08:23,465 --> 00:08:26,165
The students who finished early, comma,

162
00:08:26,185 --> 00:08:27,594
left the room.

163
00:08:27,665 --> 00:08:28,605
Wrong.

164
00:08:28,625 --> 00:08:31,645
The subject is the students
who finished early.

165
00:08:31,665 --> 00:08:33,084
The verb is left.

166
00:08:33,104 --> 00:08:34,955
No comma between them.

167
00:08:35,410 --> 00:08:37,550
Same with a verb and what follows it.

168
00:08:37,570 --> 00:08:42,140
The study found, comma,
that sleep improves memory.

169
00:08:42,170 --> 00:08:43,180
Found what?

170
00:08:43,369 --> 00:08:45,350
That sleep improves memory.

171
00:08:45,370 --> 00:08:46,500
No comma.

172
00:08:46,924 --> 00:08:51,694
And no commas around a name when the
name tells you which person you mean.

173
00:08:51,964 --> 00:08:57,424
In 1993, novelist Toni
Morrison won the Nobel Prize in

174
00:08:57,444 --> 00:08:58,444
Literature.

175
00:08:58,804 --> 00:09:03,414
No commas around Toni Morrison, because
the name tells you which novelist.

176
00:09:03,900 --> 00:09:04,990
Part two.

177
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:08,350
Form, Structure, and Sense.

178
00:09:08,780 --> 00:09:11,999
These questions test
whether words fit together:

179
00:09:12,019 --> 00:09:15,279
subjects and verbs, pronouns and nouns,

180
00:09:15,299 --> 00:09:19,270
verbs and time, and modifiers
and what they describe.

181
00:09:19,775 --> 00:09:21,185
Rule eight.

182
00:09:21,495 --> 00:09:24,595
A verb has to match its subject.

183
00:09:24,615 --> 00:09:28,035
A singular subject gets a singular verb.

184
00:09:28,055 --> 00:09:30,905
A plural subject gets a plural verb.

185
00:09:31,328 --> 00:09:35,988
The SAT makes this hard by stuffing
words between the subject and the verb.

186
00:09:36,008 --> 00:09:42,547
So cross out everything in between,
especially phrases starting with words like

187
00:09:42,567 --> 00:09:45,098
of, in, and with.

188
00:09:45,574 --> 00:09:46,584
Try this one.

189
00:09:47,094 --> 00:09:49,114
The collection of letters, photographs,

190
00:09:49,134 --> 00:09:55,104
and diaries donated to the museum by
the artist's family blank a rare look at

191
00:09:55,124 --> 00:09:56,533
her early career.

192
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:58,370
Pause and try it.

193
00:10:04,171 --> 00:10:07,311
Cross out of letters,
photographs, and diaries.

194
00:10:07,331 --> 00:10:10,711
Cross out donated to the
museum by the artist's family.

195
00:10:10,731 --> 00:10:11,941
What's left?

196
00:10:12,130 --> 00:10:15,271
The collection, blank, a rare look.

197
00:10:15,291 --> 00:10:17,060
The subject is collection.

198
00:10:17,091 --> 00:10:18,701
One collection.

199
00:10:18,810 --> 00:10:19,900
Singular.

200
00:10:20,355 --> 00:10:25,165
Offer, have offered, and
are offering are all plural.

201
00:10:25,395 --> 00:10:28,374
They're tempting because
plural nouns like letters,

202
00:10:28,394 --> 00:10:32,775
photographs, and diaries sit
between the subject and the verb.

203
00:10:32,795 --> 00:10:35,285
The answer is B, offers.

204
00:10:35,754 --> 00:10:37,164
Rule nine.

205
00:10:37,474 --> 00:10:41,164
A pronoun has to match
the noun it replaces.

206
00:10:41,234 --> 00:10:43,924
One thing gets it or its.

207
00:10:44,153 --> 00:10:46,884
More than one gets they or their.

208
00:10:47,344 --> 00:10:50,083
The SAT's favorite trap is a group noun,

209
00:10:50,103 --> 00:10:53,074
like a company, a team, or a committee.

210
00:10:53,264 --> 00:10:55,764
On the SAT, those are singular.

211
00:10:55,784 --> 00:11:01,004
After years of research, the company
finally released their first electric

212
00:11:01,024 --> 00:11:02,024
truck.

213
00:11:02,464 --> 00:11:03,674
Wrong.

214
00:11:03,784 --> 00:11:06,954
One company, so it's its
first electric truck.

215
00:11:07,410 --> 00:11:11,630
Whenever pronouns change
across the answer choices,

216
00:11:11,650 --> 00:11:16,380
find the noun the pronoun
points to, and ask one question:

217
00:11:16,930 --> 00:11:19,500
one, or more than one?

218
00:11:19,931 --> 00:11:21,181
Rule ten.

219
00:11:21,851 --> 00:11:25,671
Verb tense has to match the
timeline of the passage.

220
00:11:25,691 --> 00:11:29,591
The choices are usually the
same verb in different tenses,

221
00:11:29,611 --> 00:11:32,580
so your job is to find the time clue.

222
00:11:33,066 --> 00:11:34,066
Try this one.

223
00:11:34,586 --> 00:11:40,326
In 1903, Marie Curie became the
first woman to win a Nobel Prize.

224
00:11:40,346 --> 00:11:44,966
Eight years later, she
blank a second Nobel Prize,

225
00:11:44,986 --> 00:11:47,396
this time in chemistry.

226
00:11:47,886 --> 00:11:49,256
Pause and try it.

227
00:11:55,057 --> 00:11:58,077
The time clues are in 1903,

228
00:11:58,097 --> 00:12:00,227
and eight years later.

229
00:12:00,657 --> 00:12:05,077
That's a specific, finished
moment in the past,

230
00:12:05,097 --> 00:12:07,187
so we need the simple past.

231
00:12:07,216 --> 00:12:09,907
The answer is C, won.

232
00:12:10,436 --> 00:12:12,406
The trap is D, has won.

233
00:12:12,476 --> 00:12:15,256
That tense links the past to the present,

234
00:12:15,276 --> 00:12:19,216
so it can't go with a specific
past time like eight years later.

235
00:12:19,236 --> 00:12:23,646
Wins and will win are
the wrong time entirely.

236
00:12:24,136 --> 00:12:25,466
Rule eleven.

237
00:12:25,736 --> 00:12:28,836
Every sentence needs a real main verb.

238
00:12:28,856 --> 00:12:33,556
Words like stretching or being can't
be the main verb by themselves.

239
00:12:33,576 --> 00:12:36,945
Neither can to plus a
verb, like to stretch.

240
00:12:37,384 --> 00:12:38,434
Look at this.

241
00:12:38,464 --> 00:12:43,404
The Great Barrier Reef, stretching
more than 2,000 kilometers along the

242
00:12:43,424 --> 00:12:48,434
coast of Australia, being the largest
coral reef system in the world.

243
00:12:48,543 --> 00:12:49,603
Read it again.

244
00:12:49,623 --> 00:12:51,763
There's no main verb.

245
00:12:51,783 --> 00:12:54,954
Stretching and being can't do the job.

246
00:12:55,484 --> 00:12:59,414
Change being to is, and it works.

247
00:12:59,684 --> 00:13:03,824
The Great Barrier Reef is the largest
coral reef system in the world.

248
00:13:03,844 --> 00:13:06,943
If every choice looks fine on its own,

249
00:13:06,963 --> 00:13:10,014
check whether the sentence
even has a main verb.

250
00:13:10,584 --> 00:13:11,874
Rule twelve.

251
00:13:12,264 --> 00:13:16,084
When a sentence starts with a
describing phrase and a comma,

252
00:13:16,104 --> 00:13:20,274
the very next words have to
name the thing being described.

253
00:13:20,711 --> 00:13:23,490
Running down the street,
my backpack fell open.

254
00:13:23,510 --> 00:13:25,571
That says the backpack was running.

255
00:13:25,591 --> 00:13:30,090
The fix: Running down the street,
I felt my backpack fall open.

256
00:13:30,110 --> 00:13:32,801
Now the runner comes
right after the comma.

257
00:13:33,205 --> 00:13:36,145
Here's an SAT-style version.

258
00:13:36,165 --> 00:13:40,705
Originally developed for the
United States military blank.

259
00:13:40,725 --> 00:13:42,695
Read all four choices.

260
00:13:43,225 --> 00:13:44,595
Pause and try it.

261
00:13:51,397 --> 00:13:56,487
Ask yourself: what was originally
developed for the military?

262
00:13:56,557 --> 00:13:59,447
The Global Positioning System.

263
00:13:59,636 --> 00:14:03,696
So the Global Positioning System
has to come right after the comma.

264
00:14:03,716 --> 00:14:06,126
Only choice A does that.

265
00:14:06,538 --> 00:14:10,237
B and C put people and
the world after the comma,

266
00:14:10,257 --> 00:14:14,028
which would mean they were
developed for the military.

267
00:14:14,458 --> 00:14:18,917
D starts with there are, which
doesn't name anything at all.

268
00:14:18,937 --> 00:14:20,668
The answer is A.

269
00:14:21,124 --> 00:14:22,694
Rule thirteen.

270
00:14:22,964 --> 00:14:28,024
Apostrophes show ownership,
or a shortened word like it's.

271
00:14:28,044 --> 00:14:30,254
They never make a word plural.

272
00:14:30,754 --> 00:14:35,084
Bees, with no apostrophe,
just means more than one bee.

273
00:14:35,234 --> 00:14:38,484
Bee apostrophe s means
belonging to one bee.

274
00:14:38,674 --> 00:14:42,083
Bees apostrophe means
belonging to more than one bee.

275
00:14:42,496 --> 00:14:45,826
And the one to memorize:
it's, with an apostrophe,

276
00:14:45,855 --> 00:14:47,356
means it is.

277
00:14:47,376 --> 00:14:51,106
Its, with no apostrophe,
means belonging to it.

278
00:14:51,574 --> 00:14:52,624
Last question.

279
00:14:52,814 --> 00:14:58,234
When a honeybee finds food, she returns
to the hive and performs a waggle dance.

280
00:14:58,254 --> 00:15:03,643
The direction of the blank tells other
bees which way to fly to reach the food.

281
00:15:04,055 --> 00:15:05,425
Pause and try it.

282
00:15:11,226 --> 00:15:13,126
The passage is about one bee.

283
00:15:13,146 --> 00:15:16,486
A honeybee finds food, and
she performs the dance.

284
00:15:16,506 --> 00:15:19,236
So one bee owns the waggle run.

285
00:15:19,265 --> 00:15:21,685
That's bee apostrophe s.

286
00:15:21,705 --> 00:15:23,436
The answer is A.

287
00:15:23,917 --> 00:15:27,616
B has no apostrophe, so
nothing owns the waggle run.

288
00:15:27,636 --> 00:15:31,497
C means more than one bee, but
the passage is about just one.

289
00:15:31,517 --> 00:15:34,296
And D adds an apostrophe to waggle run,

290
00:15:34,316 --> 00:15:36,406
which doesn't own anything.

291
00:15:36,830 --> 00:15:38,690
That's all thirteen rules.

292
00:15:38,710 --> 00:15:41,210
Now, here's how to use them on test day,

293
00:15:41,230 --> 00:15:42,600
in three steps.

294
00:15:43,027 --> 00:15:44,077
Step one.

295
00:15:44,307 --> 00:15:46,806
Look at what changes between the choices.

296
00:15:46,826 --> 00:15:48,117
Punctuation?

297
00:15:48,227 --> 00:15:49,877
It's a Boundaries question.

298
00:15:49,987 --> 00:15:51,006
A verb?

299
00:15:51,026 --> 00:15:53,197
Check agreement and tense.

300
00:15:53,227 --> 00:15:54,246
A pronoun?

301
00:15:54,266 --> 00:15:56,157
Find what it refers to.

302
00:15:56,602 --> 00:15:57,652
Step two.

303
00:15:57,762 --> 00:16:01,102
Find the structure:
the complete sentences,

304
00:16:01,122 --> 00:16:04,891
the real subject, the time
clue, or who's doing the action.

305
00:16:05,384 --> 00:16:06,684
Step three.

306
00:16:06,704 --> 00:16:10,514
Eliminate using the rules, not your ear.

307
00:16:10,864 --> 00:16:13,603
If you can't name the
rule an answer breaks,

308
00:16:13,623 --> 00:16:15,154
don't cross it out yet.

309
00:16:15,605 --> 00:16:17,345
And one bonus tip.

310
00:16:17,365 --> 00:16:19,905
If two choices do the exact same job,

311
00:16:19,925 --> 00:16:23,824
like a period and a
semicolon in the same spot,

312
00:16:23,844 --> 00:16:26,144
they can't both be the one right answer.

313
00:16:26,164 --> 00:16:27,584
So neither one is.

314
00:16:27,604 --> 00:16:30,535
Look at what makes the
other choices different.

315
00:16:30,989 --> 00:16:33,368
That's all thirteen, on one screen.

316
00:16:33,388 --> 00:16:35,359
Pause here if you want a screenshot.

317
00:16:35,783 --> 00:16:39,482
Pretty much every grammar question
you'll see tests one of these.

318
